  "worked_solution": "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{-5}+Be=1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=7,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=7$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-5A{e}^{-5}+Be=7.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-5}+Be=1 \\\\ -5A{e}^{-5}+Be=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-{e}^{5},B=2{e}^{-1}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{e}^{5}\\cdot {e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5x+5}+2{e}^{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
